<%# Live /deployments board animations, driven by the DeploymentsBroadcaster Turbo
    Streams (turbo_stream_from "deployments") for every connected viewer: confetti on
    a new card, a gust on a reviewed arrival, a slide-off and grow-in on a move, mist
    on an archive, a glow on an in-place update, and a ring on a Next Release meter
    that moved. The behaviour is the board/live_fx_dom module; the event table and
    the timings are in board/live_fx. This partial carries the CSS those modules
    toggle. Only rendered on the deploy board, where the live socket is.

    #last-release is delegated to the ReleaseFx router (board/release_fx_dom), whose
    CSS tasks/_release_fx_router renders below. The two share the `.lbfx-glow` CSS
    defined in this file's <style>. %>
<%# The router's fresh-deploy CSS. Rendered from here, not from _deploy_board, so
    the two halves of the fx layer can never be included apart. %>
<%= render "tasks/release_fx_router" %>
<style>
  .lbfx-pop { animation: lbfxPop .5s cubic-bezier(.2, 1.4, .3, 1); }
  @keyframes lbfxPop { 0% { transform: scale(.85); } 55% { transform: scale(1.05); } 100% { transform: scale(1); } }

  .lbfx-reviewed-arrive { animation: lbfxReviewedArrive .7s cubic-bezier(.16, 1.2, .25, 1); }
  @keyframes lbfxReviewedArrive {
    0% { transform: translateY(8px) scale(.9); }
    58% { transform: translateY(-2px) scale(1.045); }
    100% { transform: translateY(0) scale(1); }
  }

  /* A flash, not a bloom — brightest on frame one, then fades. Colour from
     --lbfx-glow (the card's mascot type colour); falls back to a warm orange. */
  .lbfx-glow { animation: lbfxGlow .8s ease-out; }
  .lbfx-glow.lbfx-glow-stage { animation-name: lbfxGlowToStage; }
  @keyframes lbfxGlow {
    0%   { box-shadow: 0 0 18px 4px rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / .95), 0 0 44px 12px rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / .5);
           border-color: rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / 1); }
    100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / 0); border-color: rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / 0); }
  }
  @keyframes lbfxGlowToStage {
    0%   { box-shadow: 0 0 18px 4px rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / .95), 0 0 44px 12px rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / .5);
           border-color: rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / 1); }
    100% { box-shadow: var(--task-card-glow-shadow);
           border-color: var(--task-card-glow-border-color); }
  }

  .lbfx-reviewed-swell { animation: lbfxReviewedSwell .95s ease-out; }
  @keyframes lbfxReviewedSwell {
    0%   { box-shadow: 0 0 20px 5px rgb(45 212 191 / .95), 0 0 54px 16px rgb(125 211 252 / .45);
           border-color: rgb(45 212 191 / 1); }
    45%  { box-shadow: 0 0 26px 8px rgb(250 204 21 / .72), 0 0 68px 22px rgb(103 232 249 / .44);
           border-color: rgb(250 204 21 / .9); }
    100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgb(45 212 191 / 0); border-color: rgb(45 212 191 / 0); }
  }

  /* A single phase meter ticked (a test finished): ring THAT meter's BAR, don't flash the
     whole Next Release card. The engine's .studio-team-glow does the ring; this class only
     shrinks it to meter scale.

     The host is tasks/_release_phase_meter's `release-phase-glow-host` — a bare wrapper
     the exact size of the bar. Two hosts were tried and rejected, both for reasons the
     primitive states outright (engine-motion.css §9b):

     · the BAR itself — overflow-hidden (it clips the check marks), so it eats its own ring;
     · the METER WRAPPER — that boxes the label in with the bar, and at this scale the
       ring plus its bloom read as a lit blob rather than a border. Measured on screen,
       not reasoned about: it is what the first cut shipped and what the operator caught.

     Sized for an 18px bar: the engine's 4px/10px defaults are drawn for a card, and at
     meter scale the bloom is most of what you see. 2px of ring and 4px of bloom keep the
     effect on the bar.

     The bloom STAYS, chosen on screen against the alternative. The primitive's ::after is
     a blurred twin pinned at `inset: -(thickness + 4px)`, so it always reaches past the
     ring — the halo is soft-edged and does bleed toward the ASSEMBLING label. A ring-only
     cut (`::after { content: none }`) was built and viewed side by side; the haloed one is
     the one that reads. Do not "fix" the bleed by dropping ::after again — that trade was
     already made deliberately.

     No background here: the ring's ::before sits at z-index -1, and the bar's own opaque
     fill covers the host's whole interior, so only the 2px margin around it shows. */
  .release-meter-glow {
    --studio-team-glow-thickness: 2px;   /* engine default 4px */
    --studio-team-glow-bloom: 4px;       /* engine default 10px */
    --studio-team-glow-speed: 2s;        /* one full sweep across the 2s window */
  }

  /* Keep cards above active confetti. Particles render below this z-layer, so
     they peek out around the sides instead of covering card text. */
  .lbfx-confetti-active .kanban-card { position: relative; z-index: 30; }
  .lbfx-confetti-active .kanban-card.lbfx-card-front,
  .lbfx-card-front { position: relative; z-index: 40; }
</style>
<%# The behaviour is the board/live_fx_dom module (app/javascript/board), which also
    installs the ReleaseFx router. It runs once per page load and survives Turbo
    navigations; the timings and decisions it plays are in board/live_fx. %>
<%= javascript_tag 'import "board/live_fx_dom"', type: "module", nonce: true %>
